window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.machinelearning = {
  notes: [
    { title: "What ML is", body: "Machine learning means the computer finds a pattern from examples, then guesses for new examples. You show it a table of past cases. It is not a human brain and it does not understand like a person. The pattern lives in the model's numbers after training. Garbage data makes a garbage model. Start with a simple model and an honest test set. Rules you type by hand are not the same as learning from data." },
    { title: "Data first", body: "Rows are examples. Columns are features, the facts you know, plus usually one target, the answer you want to guess. Look at missing values, units, and weird rows before you fit. Most project time is cleaning, not picking a fancy model. If two columns leak the answer, scores look amazing and real life fails. Print the table head. Name the question in plain words first." },
    { title: "Train vs test", body: "Train means learn the pattern on some rows. Test means check on rows the model has not seen. If you test on train data, you cheat. A validation set is a practice test while you pick settings. The final test set stays sealed until the end. Shuffle is ok for many tables. Time data needs a time split, not a random mix of future into past." },
    { title: "Supervised learning", body: "Supervised learning means each training row has a label. Regression predicts a number. Classification predicts a category or yes/no. You train with the answer key, then you guess without it. Most beginner projects are supervised. Say which job you need before you pick a model. Metrics differ for numbers versus classes." },
    { title: "Unsupervised", body: "Unsupervised means no label. You look for groups or structure, like clustering customers. You still have to interpret the groups. There is no accuracy against y, because there is no y. k-means is the first tool people name. Plots and stories check if groups make sense. Exploration is the point, not a magic truth." },
    { title: "Overfitting", body: "Overfitting is great on train and weak on new data. The model memorized noise, like one weird house, as if it were a rule. A simpler model, more data, or regularization can help. Always compare train score and test score. A big gap is the first clue. A student who memorizes the answer key word for word fails a reworded question." },
    { title: "Underfitting", body: "Underfitting is weak on train and weak on test. The model cannot even learn the training pattern. Try a more flexible model or better features. A straight line through a rainbow curve is honest but the wrong shape. Low train score is the first clue. Do not add twenty tricks before you check this." },
    { title: "Metrics", body: "Regression uses MAE and RMSE, errors in the same units as the number. Classification uses accuracy, precision, recall, and F1. Accuracy lies when 99% of emails are not spam. A dummy model that always says not-spam looks great. Name which error hurts more in the business. Draw a confusion matrix. Pick the metric before you hunt for models." },
    { title: "Features", body: "A feature is one input the model can use. You can also build features, like price per room. Scaling to similar ranges helps many models. Do not leak the target into a feature. Fit scalers on train only, then transform test. Trees often do not need scaling. Distance models do. Write the recipe down." },
    { title: "scikit-learn flow", body: "scikit-learn is the main Python library for classic table ML. The verbs are fit, predict, and score. Split first. Then fit on train. Then predict on test. A Pipeline chains scale then model so test does not leak. Start here before deep learning. Import names are long; that is normal." },
    { title: "Bias-variance", body: "High bias means too simple, missing the real pattern. High variance means too wiggly, jumping when the training set changes. You want a sweet spot on unseen data. Regularization and more data can help variance. A richer model can help bias. A stiff ruler versus a spaghetti noodle tracing every dot. Name the trade-off in interviews." },
    { title: "Ethics", body: "Models copy bias in the data. Ask who can be harmed. A score is not fair just because it is a number. Correlation is not causation. Do not claim a model causes an outcome unless the study was built for that. Watch live scores because the world changes. Say what you would not claim." }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is machine learning?", a: "Machine learning is when a program learns from examples, not only from hand-written rules. You give it a table of past cases. It finds a pattern and then guesses for new cases.\n\nIt is not a human brain and it does not understand like a person. The pattern lives in the model's numbers after training. You still choose the data and the question.\n\nIn the code: rows are examples with size and a 0/1 label. guess(size) returns 1 if size > 50 else 0. print(guess(70)) is 1, the pricey class.\n\nA common mistake is calling any if-statement machine learning. A rule you typed by hand is not training.", code: "# rows = examples, last column = the answer (label)\nrows = [\n    [30, 0],   # 30 sqm -> cheap\n    [80, 1],   # 80 sqm -> pricey\n]\n# a \"model\" here is just a tiny rule learned from data\ndef guess(size):\n    return 1 if size > 50 else 0\nprint(guess(70))" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "AI vs ML vs deep learning?", a: "AI is the big idea: machines that act useful or smart. ML is one way to get there: learn from data. Deep learning is ML that uses neural nets with many layers.\n\nYou can do ML without deep learning, like a simple line or a tree. Nested picture: AI contains ML, ML contains deep learning.\n\nIn the code: ai includes rules, search, ml. ml includes trees, linear, deep_learning. deep_learning includes neural_nets. print says AI > ML > deep learning.\n\nA common mistake is using the three words as synonyms. Interviewers want the nested picture.", code: "# nested idea as data, not magic\nai = {\"includes\": [\"rules\", \"search\", \"ml\"]}\nml = {\"includes\": [\"trees\", \"linear\", \"deep_learning\"]}\ndeep_learning = {\"includes\": [\"neural_nets\"]}\nprint(\"AI > ML > deep learning\")\nprint(ai[\"includes\"])" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "What is a dataset?", a: "A dataset is a table of examples. Each row is one example, like one house or one email. Columns are features, the facts you know.\n\nOne column is often the label, the answer you want to guess. If rows are messy, the model will be messy too. A spreadsheet of students with marks in the last column.\n\nIn the code: pandas DataFrame has size 30, 80, 45 and price 10, 40, 18. print(df) shows the table. len(df) is 3 rows.\n\nA common mistake is fitting a model before you look at a single row. Then you miss empty cells and typos.", code: "import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"size\": [30, 80, 45],\n    \"price\": [10, 40, 18],   # label\n})\nprint(df)\nprint(\"rows\", len(df))" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "What is a feature?", a: "A feature is one input the model can use. Age, size, and word count are features. Good features often help more than a fancy model on dirty data.\n\nYou can also build features, like size per room. If you forget a useful ingredient, the dish stays bland. Ingredients you hand to a cook.\n\nIn the code: X has size and rooms. size_per_room is size divided by rooms. print(X) shows the new column.\n\nA common mistake is putting the label column into X. Then the model cheats.", code: "import pandas as pd\n# X is the feature table (inputs only)\nX = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"size\": [30, 80],\n    \"rooms\": [1, 3],\n})\nX[\"size_per_room\"] = X[\"size\"] / X[\"rooms\"]\nprint(X)" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What is a label / target?", a: "The label, or target, is the answer column. Price, spam/not spam, and cat/dog are labels. In supervised learning you have labels on the training rows.\n\nThe model never sees the label on a brand new row at predict time. You train with the key, then you take a test without it.\n\nIn the code: df has size and price. X is only size. y is price. print shows features and label separately.\n\nA common mistake is using y as a feature. That is leakage, not skill.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"size\": [30, 80, 45],\n    \"price\": [10, 40, 18],\n})\nX = df[[\"size\"]]     # features\ny = df[\"price\"]      # label / target\nprint(X)\nprint(y)" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "Supervised vs unsupervised?", a: "Supervised means each training row has a label. Unsupervised means no label; you look for groups or structure. Semi-supervised mixes a few labels with many unlabeled rows.\n\nReinforcement learning learns from rewards, like a game score. Most beginner projects are supervised. A teacher with an answer key versus sorting Lego by color with no key.\n\nIn the code: X and y are supervised pairs. customers is only X, no y. zip prints the labeled pairs. customers prints unlabeled rows.\n\nA common mistake is calling clustering classification. Classification needs labels.", code: "# supervised: X plus y\nX = [[30], [80]]\ny = [10, 40]\n# unsupervised: only X, no y\ncustomers = [[1, 20], [2, 21], [80, 5]]\nprint(\"supervised pairs\", list(zip(X, y)))\nprint(\"unsupervised rows\", customers)" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "Regression vs classification?", a: "Regression predicts a number, like house price. Classification predicts a class, like spam or not spam. Both are supervised if you have labels.\n\nThe metrics you pick later depend on this choice. Guessing a temperature versus guessing a weather word. Say which one you need before you pick a model.\n\nIn the code: price_true 40 versus price_pred 38, error is 2. spam_true and spam_pred are both 1, so the class is correct.\n\nA common mistake is accuracy on a price. Accuracy is for classes, not rupees.", code: "# regression: number\nprice_true = 40\nprice_pred = 38\n# classification: class id\nspam_true = 1\nspam_pred = 1\nprint(\"error\", abs(price_true - price_pred))\nprint(\"correct class\", spam_true == spam_pred)" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "What is training?", a: "Training is when the model changes its internal numbers. It tries to match the training labels, as scored by a loss. After training, those numbers are the learned pattern.\n\nYou do not train on the test set. Practice exams before the real exam. fit() in scikit-learn is this step.\n\nIn the code: LinearRegression fits X sizes 30, 80, 50 to prices 10, 40, 22. predict([[60]]) guesses a price for size 60.\n\nA common mistake is calling fit on the test rows. Then the test score is a cheat.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nX = [[30], [80], [50]]\ny = [10, 40, 22]\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X, y)            # training\nprint(model.predict([[60]]))" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "What is a test set?", a: "The test set is data you keep until the end. You measure real skill there. You never call fit on the test set.\n\nIf you peek too often, you start dressing for that test. Open the sealed envelope once, after you finished studying.\n\nIn the code: train is sizes 30 and 80. test is size 50 with price 22. fit uses only train. predict on X_test is compared to y_test.\n\nA common mistake is tuning twenty settings on the test set. Then test is no longer honest.", code: "# train rows vs hidden test rows\nX_train, y_train = [[30], [80]], [10, 40]\nX_test,  y_test  = [[50]], [22]\n# fit only on train\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint(m.predict(X_test), \"vs\", y_test)" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "What is a validation set?", a: "Validation is a practice test you use while choosing settings. You might try tree depth 3 vs 10 and pick the better one here. The test set stays untouched until you are done choosing.\n\nIf you tune on test, test is no longer honest. Mock tests versus the board exam. Three piles: train, valid, test.\n\nIn the code: comments draw train | train | valid | test. train is [1, 2, 3], valid [4], test [5]. prints say learn, tune, final score.\n\nA common mistake is one split named test that you also used to pick the model. That is validation wearing a test badge.", code: "# three piles (diagram as comments)\n# [ train | train | valid | test ]\ntrain, valid, test = [1, 2, 3], [4], [5]\nprint(\"learn on\", train)\nprint(\"tune on\", valid)\nprint(\"final score on\", test)" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "Why split the data?", a: "You split to see if the model works on new rows. Training score alone can look perfect while the model only memorized. A split is the cheapest honesty check.\n\nShuffle is ok for many tables; time data needs a time split. Beginners start with train_test_split. Yesterday's crossword is not today's.\n\nIn the code: X has four rows, y is 0, 0, 1, 1. test_size 0.25 and random_state 0. print shows 3 train rows and 1 test row.\n\nA common mistake is fitting on all rows, then splitting. The test rows already leaked into the model.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\nX = [[1], [2], [3], [4]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nXtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(\n    X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=0\n)\nprint(len(Xtr), len(Xte))" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "What is overfitting?", a: "Overfitting is great on train and weak on new data. The model learned noise, like one weird house, as if it were a rule. A simpler model, more data, or regularization can help.\n\nAlways compare train score and test score. A student who memorizes the answer key word for word fails a reworded question.\n\nIn the code: train_acc 0.99 and test_acc 0.55. gap is 0.44. If the gap is > 0.2, the snippet prints likely overfitting.\n\nA common mistake is celebrating 99% train accuracy with no test number.", code: "# train score high, test score low = overfit picture\ntrain_acc = 0.99\ntest_acc = 0.55\nprint(\"gap\", train_acc - test_acc)\nif train_acc - test_acc > 0.2:\n    print(\"likely overfitting\")" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "What is underfitting?", a: "Underfitting is weak on train and weak on test. The model cannot even learn the training pattern. Try a more flexible model or better features.\n\nA straight line through a rainbow curve is honest, but it is the wrong shape. Low train score is the first clue.\n\nIn the code: train_acc 0.52 and test_acc 0.50. print says both low -> underfit. The comment suggests a richer model or better features.\n\nA common mistake is adding dropout and other regularizers when the model is already too weak. That can make underfit worse.", code: "train_acc = 0.52\ntest_acc = 0.50\nprint(\"both low -> underfit\")\n# a richer model or better features may lift both" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "What is a baseline?", a: "A baseline is a simple guess, like always predict the average. Or always predict the most common class. If your model cannot beat this, it is not useful yet.\n\nBaselines keep you honest. Report the baseline next to your fancy score. A coin flip or everyone gets B as the first teacher.\n\nIn the code: y is 10, 40, 18, 20. baseline is the mean. MAE is the mean of absolute errors against that mean.\n\nA common mistake is skipping the baseline. Then 0.60 accuracy might still be worse than always guessing the common class.", code: "import numpy as np\ny = np.array([10, 40, 18, 20])\nbaseline = y.mean()          # always guess the average\nprint(\"baseline price\", baseline)\nerrors = np.abs(y - baseline)\nprint(\"MAE\", errors.mean())" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "What is accuracy?", a: "Accuracy is the share of guesses that are exactly right. It is easy to say in one sentence. It lies when classes are imbalanced, like 99% not-spam.\n\nA dummy model that always says not-spam gets 99%. Always saying no tornado in a quiet town looks accurate and is useless. Use other metrics when one class is rare.\n\nIn the code: true is 0, 0, 0, 1. pred is always 0. accuracy is 0.75 and misses all spam.\n\nA common mistake is reporting only accuracy on fraud. The model can ignore fraud and still look good.", code: "true = [0, 0, 0, 1]      # 1 is rare spam\npred = [0, 0, 0, 0]      # always not spam\nok = sum(t == p for t, p in zip(true, pred))\nprint(\"accuracy\", ok / len(true))   # 0.75, misses all spam" },
    { id: 16, level: "beginner", q: "Precision vs recall?", a: "Precision: of the rows you called spam, how many really were spam? Recall: of all real spam, how many did you catch? There is a trade-off if you move the yes/no cutoff.\n\nName which error hurts more in the business. Airport security: flagged bags that had a problem versus real problem bags that you found.\n\nIn the code: true 1, 1, 0, 0 and pred 1, 0, 1, 0. tp 1, fp 1, fn 1. precision and recall both print 0.5.\n\nA common mistake is mixing the two words. Precision is about your yes guesses. Recall is about the real yes rows.", code: "# 1 = spam\ntrue = [1, 1, 0, 0]\npred = [1, 0, 1, 0]\n# precision: 1 of 2 flagged = 0.5\n# recall:    1 of 2 real spam = 0.5\ntp = 1; fp = 1; fn = 1\nprint(\"precision\", tp / (tp + fp))\nprint(\"recall\", tp / (tp + fn))" },
    { id: 17, level: "beginner", q: "What is F1?", a: "F1 mixes precision and recall with a harmonic mean. It is useful when you care about both and classes are uneven. It is still one number, so it can hide details.\n\nLook at the confusion matrix too. If either precision or recall is 0, F1 is 0. A grade that punishes being bad at either homework or the exam.\n\nIn the code: p and r are 0.5, F1 is 0.5. The comment says if recall is 0, F1 is 0.\n\nA common mistake is quoting F1 without saying which class it is for. The rare class is usually the one you meant.", code: "p, r = 0.5, 0.5\nf1 = 2 * p * r / (p + r)\nprint(\"F1\", f1)\n\np2, r2 = 1.0, 0.0\n# recall 0 -> F1 0 (do not divide; just know the rule)\nprint(\"if recall is 0, F1 is 0\")" },
    { id: 18, level: "beginner", q: "Confusion matrix?", a: "Rows are the truth. Columns are the guess, or the other way; say which. You see true yes, true no, false alarms, and misses.\n\nAccuracy is just the diagonal over the total. Always label the axes so nobody mixes FP and FN. A 2-by-2 scoreboard.\n\nIn the code: comments draw TN FP / FN TP. tn 50, fp 5, fn 10, tp 35. print shows accuracy and the two error counts.\n\nA common mistake is swapping FP and FN in the story. Then the business cost is backwards.", code: "#          pred 0  pred 1\n# true 0     TN      FP\n# true 1     FN      TP\ntn, fp, fn, tp = 50, 5, 10, 35\nprint(\"accuracy\", (tp + tn) / (tp + tn + fp + fn))\nprint(\"false alarms FP\", fp)\nprint(\"misses FN\", fn)" },
    { id: 19, level: "beginner", q: "MAE vs RMSE?", a: "MAE is the average absolute miss, easy to explain in rupees. RMSE squares misses first, so big mistakes hurt more. Both are for numbers, not for classes.\n\nPick MAE if you want a typical miss. Pick RMSE if huge misses are extra bad. 1 km late versus 100 km late on a trip.\n\nIn the code: true 10, 20, 30 and pred 12, 20, 40. MAE averages the absolute misses. RMSE is the square root of mean squared misses.\n\nA common mistake is RMSE on a classification problem. You need class metrics there.", code: "import numpy as np\ntrue = np.array([10.0, 20.0, 30.0])\npred = np.array([12.0, 20.0, 40.0])\nmae = np.abs(true - pred).mean()\nrmse = np.sqrt(((true - pred) ** 2).mean())\nprint(\"MAE\", mae)\nprint(\"RMSE\", rmse)" },
    { id: 20, level: "beginner", q: "What is linear regression?", a: "Linear regression fits y ≈ w*x + b, or more features. It is simple, fast, and a good first model for a number. Weights tell you how each feature pushes the answer.\n\nIt can underfit curved patterns. Stretching a ruler through a cloud of dots. Start here before a forest of trees.\n\nIn the code: X is 1, 2, 3 and y is 2, 4, 6. After fit, slope and intercept print. predict([[4]]) continues the line.\n\nA common mistake is a linear model on a clearly curved plot. The ruler cannot become a rainbow.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX = [[1], [2], [3]]\ny = [2, 4, 6]\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint(\"slope\", m.coef_[0], \"intercept\", m.intercept_)\nprint(m.predict([[4]]))" },
    { id: 21, level: "beginner", q: "What is logistic regression?", a: "Despite the name, logistic regression is for classification. It outputs a probability between 0 and 1. Then you pick a cutoff, often 0.5, to say yes or no.\n\nIt is a common first model for spam or pass/fail. A volume knob from no to yes, not a price. The name confuses everyone once; then you remember.\n\nIn the code: X is 1, 2, 10, 11 and y is 0, 0, 1, 1. predict_proba for 3 prints two probabilities. predict prints the class.\n\nA common mistake is treating the probability as a guaranteed percent. It is a model score, not a promise.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nm = LogisticRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint(m.predict_proba([[3]])[0])   # [p_class0, p_class1]\nprint(m.predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 22, level: "beginner", q: "What is a decision tree?", a: "A decision tree is a flowchart of yes/no questions on features. It is easy to explain to a person. If it grows too deep, it memorizes and overfits.\n\nYou can cap the depth. A clinic form: if fever and cough then. One tree is the story; a forest is many stories voting.\n\nIn the code: DecisionTreeClassifier max_depth 2 fits the same 0/1 sizes. predict([[3]]) follows the learned splits.\n\nA common mistake is max_depth None on a small table. The tree then memorizes every row.", code: "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nt = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=2, random_state=0)\nt.fit(X, y)\nprint(t.predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 23, level: "beginner", q: "What is k-nearest neighbors?", a: "To guess a new point, look at the k closest training points. For classes they vote. For numbers they average. It is simple to say and heavy if the table is huge.\n\nDistance cares about feature scale, so scale first. You store the training set; there is little training math. Asking the k closest houses what they sold for.\n\nIn the code: KNeighborsClassifier n_neighbors 3 fits sizes 1, 2, 10, 11. predict([[3]]) asks the nearest three labels.\n\nA common mistake is k-NN on unscaled income and age. Income in rupees then dominates the distance.", code: "from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nknn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)\nknn.fit(X, y)\nprint(knn.predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 24, level: "beginner", q: "What is k-means?", a: "k-means puts rows into k groups. You pick k. It moves group centers until they settle. It cares about scale and random start points.\n\nThere is no correct k from math alone. You still have to name what a group means. Placing k picnic blankets and pulling people to the nearest blanket.\n\nIn the code: four points, two near origin and two near 8,8. KMeans n_clusters 2 fit_predict prints a 0/1 group id per row.\n\nA common mistake is treating cluster ids as true labels. They are groups you still have to explain.", code: "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nX = [[1, 1], [1, 2], [8, 8], [9, 8]]\nkm = KMeans(n_clusters=2, n_init=10, random_state=0)\nprint(km.fit_predict(X))" },
    { id: 25, level: "beginner", q: "What is a neural network in one sentence?", a: "A neural network is layers of simple math units that transform features. With many layers people call it deep learning. It needs more data and more tuning than a linear model.\n\nEach machine mixes the last outputs a bit. Beginners can start with sklearn, then nets later. A factory with many small machines in a row.\n\nIn the code: x is 0.5. hidden is ReLU of w1 * x. out is w2 * hidden. print shows that tiny forward pass.\n\nA common mistake is a huge net on 40 rows. The net memorizes and you learn nothing useful.", code: "# tiny picture: input -> hidden -> output\nx = 0.5\nw1, w2 = 0.8, -0.2\nhidden = max(0, w1 * x)        # ReLU\nout = w2 * hidden\nprint(\"output\", out)" },
    { id: 26, level: "beginner", q: "What is a loss function?", a: "A loss is a number for how wrong. Training tries to make this number smaller. Mean squared error is a common loss for numbers.\n\nCross-entropy is common for classes. If the loss is the wrong kind, the model learns the wrong goal. A score in a game you want to drive down, not up.\n\nIn the code: true 3 and 5, pred 2 and 5. mse is the mean of squared differences. print shows that loss.\n\nA common mistake is minimizing accuracy as if it were a smooth loss. Accuracy does not give a useful slope.", code: "import numpy as np\ntrue = np.array([3.0, 5.0])\npred = np.array([2.0, 5.0])\nmse = ((true - pred) ** 2).mean()\nprint(\"MSE loss\", mse)" },
    { id: 27, level: "beginner", q: "What is gradient descent?", a: "Imagine the loss as a hilly landscape. Gradient descent takes a small step downhill and repeats. That is how neural nets and many linear models move weights.\n\nToo big a step jumps over the valley. You do not need to write it by hand in sklearn. Walking down a foggy hill with small careful steps.\n\nIn the code: loss is (w - 4)**2 so the bottom is at 4. Each step uses grad 2*(w-4) and lr 0.2. print shows w walking toward 4.\n\nA common mistake is a learning rate so big that w bounces away from the bottom.", code: "# one weight, loss = (w - 4)**2, downhill toward 4\nw = 0.0\nfor step in range(5):\n    grad = 2 * (w - 4)     # slope\n    w = w - 0.2 * grad     # small step\n    print(step, w)" },
    { id: 28, level: "beginner", q: "What is a learning rate?", a: "The learning rate is how big each downhill step is. Too big: you bounce and miss the bottom. Too small: you crawl and training takes forever.\n\nIt is a common hyperparameter to try a few values. Libraries often pick a decent default. Giant leaps versus baby steps on a hill.\n\nIn the code: step(0, 2.0) uses a big lr from w=0. step(0, 0.05) uses a small lr. print compares those next w values.\n\nA common mistake is changing the model family before trying two learning rates. The dial was the bug.", code: "def step(w, lr):\n    grad = 2 * (w - 4)\n    return w - lr * grad\n\nprint(\"big lr\", step(0, 2.0))\nprint(\"small lr\", step(0, 0.05))" },
    { id: 29, level: "beginner", q: "What is a hyperparameter?", a: "A hyperparameter is a setting not learned as a usual weight. k in k-NN, tree depth, and learning rate are examples. You tune them with validation, not with the test set.\n\nToo many tries on test is cheating. Write down what you tried. Oven temperature: you choose it; the cake batter does not.\n\nIn the code: a loop tries max_depth 1, 2, and 4. Each DecisionTreeClassifier is built with that depth. print says trying depth.\n\nA common mistake is picking the depth that won on the test set. Then you have no honest final number.", code: "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n# max_depth is a hyperparameter you choose\nfor depth in [1, 2, 4]:\n    t = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=depth)\n    print(\"trying depth\", depth, t)" },
    { id: 30, level: "beginner", q: "What is cross-validation?", a: "You split train into folds. Train on some folds, score on the held fold, then rotate. k=5 is common.\n\nIt is slower than one split and more stable on small data. The test set can still wait at the end. Five mock exams, each leaving out a different chapter.\n\nIn the code: six tiny rows, three 0 then three 1. cross_val_score with cv=3 prints three fold scores for LogisticRegression.\n\nA common mistake is putting the test set inside cross-validation. Then you have no sealed final exam.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nX = [[1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6]]\ny = [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1]\nm = LogisticRegression()\nprint(cross_val_score(m, X, y, cv=3))" },
    { id: 31, level: "beginner", q: "Why scale features?", a: "Distance-based models listen to the big-number column. StandardScaler or MinMaxScaler puts features on a similar scale. Fit the scaler on train only.\n\nTrees often do not need this. k-NN and linear models often do. Height in km and weight in grams; one number shouts.\n\nIn the code: X_train has 1 and 1000, then 2 and 2000. StandardScaler fits train only. transform on X_test 1.5, 1500 uses those train stats.\n\nA common mistake is fitting the scaler on train plus test. Then test leaked into the scale.", code: "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nX_train = [[1.0, 1000.0], [2.0, 2000.0]]\nX_test = [[1.5, 1500.0]]\nsc = StandardScaler().fit(X_train)   # train only\nprint(sc.transform(X_test))" },
    { id: 32, level: "beginner", q: "When do you not need scaling?", a: "Decision trees, random forests, and many boosting trees split on thresholds. A split income > 100000 works without scaling. Linear models, k-NN, SVM, and neural nets often want scaled inputs.\n\nStill scale if you mix models in one pipeline. A flowchart that asks bigger than this number does not care about other units.\n\nIn the code: a tree fits unscaled X with a 1000-scale second column. predict([[2, 2500]]) still runs.\n\nA common mistake is scaling because a blog said always scale, then using only trees. It is extra work, not always harm, but it is not required.", code: "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nX = [[1, 1000], [2, 2000], [3, 3000]]\ny = [0, 0, 1]\n# trees can fit unscaled X\nprint(DecisionTreeClassifier().fit(X, y).predict([[2, 2500]]))" },
    { id: 33, level: "beginner", q: "What is one-hot encoding?", a: "Red/blue/green becomes columns of 0 and 1. Linear models need numbers; they cannot multiply blue. Trees can be more flexible, but one-hot is still common.\n\nFit the encoder on train so new colors in test are handled on purpose. Three light switches, only one on. Do not encode the label this way into X; that would leak.\n\nIn the code: OneHotEncoder sparse_output False fits red, blue, red. fit_transform prints 0/1 columns. categories_ lists the colors.\n\nA common mistake is fitting the encoder on all data including test. A rare test-only color then leaks into the columns.", code: "from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\nenc = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False)\nX = [[\"red\"], [\"blue\"], [\"red\"]]\nprint(enc.fit_transform(X))\nprint(enc.categories_)" },
    { id: 34, level: "beginner", q: "What is missing data?", a: "Missing is NaN or blank. You can drop the row, fill with median or mode, or use a model that allows NaN. Fit the imputer on train only.\n\nIf you compute the fill value on train+test together, you leaked. Guessing using tomorrow's answers is cheating. A form with skipped questions.\n\nIn the code: X_train ages 20, None, 30. SimpleImputer median fits train. transform on a test None fills with the train median.\n\nA common mistake is filling missing with the global median including test. That is contamination.", code: "import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nX_train = pd.DataFrame({\"age\": [20, None, 30]})\nX_test = pd.DataFrame({\"age\": [None]})\nimp = SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\").fit(X_train)\nprint(imp.transform(X_test))" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "What is data leakage?", a: "Leakage is information the model would not have at predict time. Examples: future columns, or the target hiding inside a feature. Scores look amazing. Real life fails.\n\nAsk: would I know this field when I must guess? If no, drop it. Seeing the exam answer printed in the margin of the question.\n\nIn the code: df has size and sold_price. X uses only size. The comment says sold_price is the target, not a feature.\n\nA common mistake is a feature like days_until_churn that was built with the churn date. That peeks at the future.", code: "import pandas as pd\n# leak: sold_price is the target, not a feature\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"size\": [80],\n    \"sold_price\": [40],\n})\nX = df[[\"size\"]]   # honest features only\nprint(X)" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "Train/test contamination?", a: "If you fit a scaler on all rows including test, test leaked. Split first. Then fit transforms on train. Then transform test.\n\nPipelines do this in one object. Always say split first. Practicing with the answer key mixed into the flashcards.\n\nIn the code: train_test_split first. StandardScaler.fit(Xtr) not on Xte. Xte_s is transform of test only.\n\nA common mistake is scaler.fit(X) on the whole matrix, then split. The test mean already entered the scale.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nX, y = [[1], [2], [100], [101]], [0, 0, 1, 1]\nXtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=0)\nsc = StandardScaler().fit(Xtr)     # not on Xte\nXte_s = sc.transform(Xte)\nprint(Xte_s)" },
    { id: 37, level: "beginner", q: "What is scikit-learn?", a: "scikit-learn is the main Python library for classic ML. You get split, models, metrics, and pipelines. Start here before deep learning.\n\nThe style is fit, predict, score. Import names are long; that is normal. A well-labeled toolbox for table data.\n\nIn the code: it imports train_test_split and LogisticRegression. print names split, model, metric. print(LogisticRegression) shows the class.\n\nA common mistake is jumping to a huge neural net for a 200-row spreadsheet. sklearn is the right first toolbox.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nprint(\"split, model, metric — sklearn\")\nprint(LogisticRegression)" },
    { id: 38, level: "beginner", q: "fit, predict, score?", a: "fit learns from X and y. predict guesses y for new X. score is a quick metric, often accuracy or R². That pattern repeats for most models.\n\nMemorize these three words. Study, then exam, then a red pen.\n\nIn the code: LinearRegression fits [[1],[2]] to [2,4]. predict([[3]]) continues the line. score on the train pairs prints a fit quality number.\n\nA common mistake is calling score on the same rows you just fit and treating that as the real skill. Check test too.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression()\nm.fit([[1], [2]], [2, 4])\nprint(m.predict([[3]]))\nprint(m.score([[1], [2]], [2, 4]))" },
    { id: 39, level: "beginner", q: "What is a pipeline?", a: "A pipeline is one object with steps inside, like scale then model. It fits each step on train and applies the same to new data. It prevents the I scaled the whole dataset leak.\n\nUse it in interviews; it shows care. You still split data first. A factory belt: wash, then bake, in that order every time.\n\nIn the code: Pipeline has StandardScaler then LogisticRegression. fit on [[1.0],[10.0]] with labels 0, 1. predict([[4.0]]) runs scale then model.\n\nA common mistake is scaling by hand on all rows, then putting only the model in a pipeline. The leak already happened.", code: "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\npipe = Pipeline([\n    (\"scale\", StandardScaler()),\n    (\"model\", LogisticRegression()),\n])\npipe.fit([[1.0], [10.0]], [0, 1])\nprint(pipe.predict([[4.0]]))" },
    { id: 40, level: "beginner", q: "What is pandas used for in ML?", a: "You load CSV, clean columns, filter rows, and make features. The table is what you understand before the model. You then pass arrays or DataFrames into sklearn.\n\nMost real time is here, not in picking XGBoost. Print df.head() in your head. A kitchen prep board before the oven.\n\nIn the code: DataFrame size 30, 80 and price 10, 40. head prints the table. mean of size prints. X is size, y is price.\n\nA common mistake is fit() before head() and describe(). Then you never saw the empty cells.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"size\": [30, 80], \"price\": [10, 40]})\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df[\"size\"].mean())\nX = df[[\"size\"]]\ny = df[\"price\"]" },
    { id: 41, level: "beginner", q: "What is NumPy?", a: "NumPy is fast arrays of numbers. Models use arrays under the hood. pandas sits on top for named columns.\n\nYou need it for metrics you write yourself. shape tells you rows and columns. A grid of numbers with no extra labels.\n\nIn the code: X is a 2x2 array 1,2 / 3,4. shape prints (2, 2). mean prints the average of all four numbers.\n\nA common mistake is looping Python lists for huge numeric work. NumPy is built for that grid.", code: "import numpy as np\nX = np.array([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])\nprint(X.shape)\nprint(X.mean())" },
    { id: 42, level: "beginner", q: "What is an epoch?", a: "An epoch is one full walk over the training rows. Neural nets often need many epochs. Too many epochs can overfit.\n\nsklearn linear models often solve without this word. You will hear it in deep learning. Reading the textbook once per epoch.\n\nIn the code: rows 1, 2, 3. Two epochs each visit every row. print finished epoch 0 then 1.\n\nA common mistake is 1000 epochs on a tiny net with no validation check. Train loss falls while real skill dies.", code: "# epoch picture\nrows = [1, 2, 3]\nfor epoch in range(2):\n    for row in rows:\n        pass\n    print(\"finished epoch\", epoch)" },
    { id: 43, level: "beginner", q: "What is a batch?", a: "A batch is a small group of rows for one gradient step. Batch size is a hyperparameter. Mini-batch is the usual default in nets.\n\nFull batch uses all rows at once; that can be heavy. You will see batch in PyTorch later. Grading five papers, then moving the red pen rule a bit.\n\nIn the code: rows 0 through 5, batch_size 2. The loop slices [0:2], [2:4], [4:6] and prints each batch.\n\nA common mistake is batch size equal to the whole dataset on a GPU that cannot hold it. Then training crashes.", code: "rows = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nbatch_size = 2\nfor i in range(0, len(rows), batch_size):\n    batch = rows[i:i + batch_size]\n    print(\"batch\", batch)" },
    { id: 44, level: "intermediate", q: "Bias-variance trade-off?", a: "High bias: too simple, misses the real pattern, underfit. High variance: too wiggly, jumps when the training set changes, overfit. You want a sweet spot on unseen data.\n\nRegularization and more data can help variance. A richer model can help bias. A stiff ruler versus a spaghetti noodle tracing every dot.\n\nIn the code: simple maps high bias low variance. wiggly maps low bias high variance. print shows both pictures.\n\nA common mistake is saying bias when you mean a prejudiced dataset. Here bias is systematic error of a too-simple model.", code: "# picture, not a proof\nsimple = {\"bias\": \"high\", \"variance\": \"low\"}\nwiggly = {\"bias\": \"low\", \"variance\": \"high\"}\nprint(simple)\nprint(wiggly)" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "What is regularization?", a: "Regularization adds a penalty on large weights. The model stays a bit simpler. It fights overfitting in linear models and nets.\n\nL1 and L2 are two common speed limits. You still need a validation set to pick the strength. A speed limit on how dramatic a weight can be.\n\nIn the code: y has a noisy last point 8. Ridge alpha 1.0 fits X 1..4. print shows the shrunk coefficient.\n\nA common mistake is alpha so huge that every weight is nearly 0. Then you underfit on purpose.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nX = [[1], [2], [3], [4]]\ny = [1, 2, 2.5, 8]     # last point is noisy\nm = Ridge(alpha=1.0).fit(X, y)\nprint(m.coef_)" },
    { id: 46, level: "intermediate", q: "L1 vs L2?", a: "L2 Ridge shrinks weights smoothly. L1 Lasso can push some weights to zero and drop features. Elastic net mixes both.\n\nUse L1 when you want a sparse story. Use L2 as a calm default. Turning all volume knobs down a bit versus clicking some off.\n\nIn the code: Ridge and Lasso fit the same tiny X and y. print shows both coef_ arrays so you can compare zeros versus shrinks.\n\nA common mistake is calling L1 and L2 loss functions. Here they are penalties on weights, not the data loss itself.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import Lasso, Ridge\nX = [[1, 0], [2, 0], [3, 1]]\ny = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(\"ridge\", Ridge(alpha=0.5).fit(X, y).coef_)\nprint(\"lasso\", Lasso(alpha=0.1).fit(X, y).coef_)" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a random forest?", a: "A random forest is many decision trees on random slices of rows and features. They vote or average. It is a strong default for tables.\n\nIt is harder to explain than one tree. You still should not skip a baseline. A panel of doctors, each seeing a slightly different file.\n\nIn the code: RandomForestClassifier n_estimators 10 fits the 0/1 sizes. predict([[3]]) is the vote of those trees.\n\nA common mistake is 1000 trees on 40 rows. You wait a long time for a model that still overfits noise.", code: "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=10, random_state=0)\nrf.fit(X, y)\nprint(rf.predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "What is gradient boosting?", a: "Trees are added one by one. Each new tree focuses on leftover error. XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost are famous names.\n\nThey often win on spreadsheet-style data. They can overfit if you add too many trees. A student correcting yesterday's wrong answers, then the new leftovers.\n\nIn the code: GradientBoostingClassifier fits the same tiny 0/1 data. predict([[3]]) uses the sequence of trees.\n\nA common mistake is boosting until train accuracy is 100% with no validation. Extra trees then memorize noise.", code: "from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingClassifier\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\ngb = GradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=0)\ngb.fit(X, y)\nprint(gb.predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 49, level: "intermediate", q: "What is SVM in one line?", a: "SVM looks for a wide street, a margin, between classes. Kernels can bend that street for curved borders. It can be slow on huge data.\n\nPoints on the edge are support vectors. Scale features first. Drawing the fattest safe road between two villages.\n\nIn the code: SVC fits sizes 1, 2, 10, 11 with labels 0, 0, 1, 1. predict([[3]]) uses that margin.\n\nA common mistake is SVM on unscaled features with very different units. The wide street then follows the loud column.", code: "from sklearn.svm import SVC\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nprint(SVC().fit(X, y).predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "What is Naive Bayes?", a: "Naive Bayes uses Bayes' rule and pretends features are independent. That independence idea is the naive part. It is fast and strong for spam word counts.\n\nWrong assumption, often still useful. Great baseline for text. Guessing weather from cloud and wind as if they do not affect each other.\n\nIn the code: GaussianNB fits the same tiny 0/1 sizes. predict([[3]]) prints the class.\n\nA common mistake is expecting it to model feature interactions well. The independence shortcut is the point and the limit.", code: "from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\nX = [[1], [2], [10], [11]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nprint(GaussianNB().fit(X, y).predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 51, level: "intermediate", q: "What is PCA?", a: "PCA rotates features to new axes that capture the most spread. It is unsupervised compression or a 2D plot trick. It is not a supervised pick the best features for y by itself.\n\nScale first. You lose some detail on purpose. Photographing a 3D sculpture from the angle that shows the most shape.\n\nIn the code: four 2D points go into PCA n_components 1. fit_transform prints shape with one column left.\n\nA common mistake is PCA to 'improve accuracy' without checking whether the dropped axes held the label signal.", code: "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nX = [[1, 1], [2, 2], [3, 3], [1, 2]]\np = PCA(n_components=1).fit_transform(X)\nprint(p.shape)" },
    { id: 52, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a confusion between correlation and causation?", a: "Correlation means they move together. Causation means one makes the other happen. Ice cream and drowning both rise in summer.\n\nML finds correlation unless you design a careful study. Be humble in business claims. Two clocks that both say 3; neither clock causes the hour.\n\nIn the code: ice_cream 2, 4, 8 and drowning 1, 2, 4. zip shows they rise together. The comment names summer as the hidden cause.\n\nA common mistake is telling a manager the model proves X causes Y because the weight is positive.", code: "ice_cream = [2, 4, 8]\ndrowning = [1, 2, 4]\n# they rise together; summer is the hidden cause\nprint(list(zip(ice_cream, drowning)))" },
    { id: 53, level: "intermediate", q: "Imbalanced classes?", a: "Fraud and disease are often rare. Accuracy lies. Use precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, class weights, or careful resampling.\n\nThink about the cost of a miss. Always saying healthy is 99.9% accurate and useless. Talk about the rare class first.\n\nIn the code: y is 99 zeros and one 1. always-0 accuracy is 0.99. The comment says look at recall on class 1 instead.\n\nA common mistake is celebrating 99% accuracy on that table. You caught zero of the rare class.", code: "y = [0] * 99 + [1]\nprint(\"always-0 accuracy\", y.count(0) / len(y))\n# better: look at recall on class 1, not this number\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "ROC-AUC?", a: "ROC-AUC scores how well the model ranks positives above negatives across cutoffs. 0.5 is a coin flip. 1.0 is perfect ranking.\n\nYou still pick a real threshold for production. Not the only metric if one cutoff matters. Lining people up by how spammy and seeing if real spam is near the front.\n\nIn the code: y_true 0, 0, 1, 1 and y_score 0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8. roc_auc_score prints that ranking number.\n\nA common mistake is quoting AUC and never choosing a threshold. Production still needs a yes/no rule.", code: "from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\ny_true = [0, 0, 1, 1]\ny_score = [0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8]\nprint(roc_auc_score(y_true, y_score))" },
    { id: 55, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a threshold?", a: "If probability > 0.5, say yes — that 0.5 is a threshold. Lower it to catch more spam, more false alarms. Raise it to flag fewer people, more misses.\n\nPick the dial using costs, not habit. Validation data helps you choose. A smoke alarm sensitivity dial.\n\nIn the code: probs 0.2, 0.49, 0.51, 0.9. thr 0.5. pred is 0, 0, 1, 1 because only the last two meet the cutoff.\n\nA common mistake is leaving 0.5 forever when misses cost 100 times more than false alarms.", code: "probs = [0.2, 0.49, 0.51, 0.9]\nthr = 0.5\npred = [1 if p >= thr else 0 for p in probs]\nprint(pred)" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "What is feature engineering?", a: "You turn a date into day of week, text into word counts, price into price per area. This often beats switching models. Do it on train, then apply the same recipe to test.\n\nWrite the recipe down. Raw columns are not always the best ingredients. Chopping vegetables so the cook can actually cook.\n\nIn the code: area 50, 100 and price 10, 40. price_per_area is price / area. print shows the new column.\n\nA common mistake is building a feature with the test label mixed in. Then you leaked.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"area\": [50, 100], \"price\": [10, 40]})\ndf[\"price_per_area\"] = df[\"price\"] / df[\"area\"]\nprint(df)" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "What is feature selection?", a: "You remove noisy or useless features. It can speed training and reduce overfit. Do it with train/validation, never using test labels to pick.\n\nToo much dropping can throw away a real signal. A model with L1 can also shrink features for you. Packing a suitcase: leave the bricks at home.\n\nIn the code: df has a, noise, y. X keeps only a. print shows the reduced table.\n\nA common mistake is picking features using the test set's labels. That is another leak.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"a\": [1, 2], \"noise\": [9, 9], \"y\": [0, 1]})\nX = df[[\"a\"]]          # dropped noise on purpose\nprint(X)" },
    { id: 58, level: "intermediate", q: "Outliers?", a: "Outliers are far-away points. They can pull a mean or a linear model. Check if they are typing errors or real rare events before you delete them.\n\nDeleting real rare events can hide the thing you care about, like fraud. Plot first. One mansion in a street of small houses.\n\nIn the code: x is 10, 11, 12, 1000. mean is pulled up. median resists the 1000.\n\nA common mistake is dropping every extreme automatically. Fraud is often the extreme.", code: "import numpy as np\nx = np.array([10, 11, 12, 1000])\nprint(\"mean\", x.mean())\nprint(\"median\", np.median(x))   # median resists the 1000" },
    { id: 59, level: "beginner", q: "What is EDA?", a: "EDA is exploratory data analysis. You look at shapes, missing values, simple plots, and weird rows. It saves weeks of confusion.\n\nhead, describe, and a histogram are enough to start. Do this before fit(). Tasting the soup before adding more spice.\n\nIn the code: df ages 20, 21, None, 80. head prints rows. describe prints stats. isna().sum() counts the missing age.\n\nA common mistake is fit() on day one. Then you spend a week debugging a typo you would have seen in head().", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"age\": [20, 21, None, 80]})\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.describe())\nprint(df.isna().sum())" },
    { id: 60, level: "beginner", q: "Why plot the data?", a: "A scatter or histogram shows outliers, a bad join, and whether a line even makes sense. Do this before fit(). Averages hide two clumps.\n\nYou do not need a pretty dashboard; a simple plot is enough. Then you pick a model that matches the shape. Looking at a map instead of only the average GPS number.\n\nIn the code: xs is 1, 2, 3 and 80, 81, 82. min max mean print. The comment says the mean sits in the empty middle.\n\nA common mistake is trusting the mean alone. Two groups look like one boring average.", code: "# picture in numbers: two clumps\nxs = [1, 2, 3, 80, 81, 82]\nprint(\"min\", min(xs), \"max\", max(xs), \"mean\", sum(xs)/len(xs))\n# mean sits in the empty middle — a plot would show two groups" },
    { id: 61, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a holdout set?", a: "Holdout is one train/test cut. It is fast. Cross-validation is more work and more stable when data is small.\n\nUse holdout for a first look. Keep a final test if you can. One mock exam versus five rotating mock exams.\n\nIn the code: four rows, test_size 0.25, random_state 0. print holdout size is how many rows landed in Xho.\n\nA common mistake is a 50/50 split on 40 rows. Train then has too little to learn.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nX, y = [[1], [2], [3], [4]], [0, 0, 1, 1]\nXtr, Xho, ytr, yho = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=0)\nprint(\"holdout size\", len(Xho))" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "Time-based split?", a: "If rows have a date, like sales, do not random-shuffle. Train on the past. Test on later days. A random split leaks the future.\n\nTime series needs this honesty. Say it out loud in case studies. Predicting tomorrow using a newspaper from next week.\n\nIn the code: days 1 to 4 with sales. train is day <= 3. test is day > 3. print shows past then future.\n\nA common mistake is train_test_split shuffle on dated sales. The model then sees next week's pattern in train.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"day\": [1, 2, 3, 4],\n    \"sales\": [10, 12, 11, 20],\n})\ntrain = df[df[\"day\"] <= 3]\ntest = df[df[\"day\"] > 3]\nprint(train)\nprint(test)" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a random seed?", a: "A seed makes random splits repeatable. 42 is a joke default, not magic quality. It helps you and a teacher compare the same rows.\n\nIn real life you still need an honest future/test plan. It is for debugging, not for better models. The same shuffle of cards to replay a lesson.\n\nIn the code: two train_test_split calls with random_state 42. print(a[0] == b[0]) is True because the shuffle matches.\n\nA common mistake is thinking 42 makes the model smarter. It only makes the split repeatable.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nX, y = [[1], [2], [3], [4]], [0, 1, 0, 1]\na = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=42)\nb = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=42)\nprint(a[0] == b[0])" },
    { id: 64, level: "advanced", q: "What is the curse of dimensionality?", a: "With many features and few rows, distance becomes less meaningful. Models overfit empty space. You need more data, fewer features, or regularization.\n\nEach extra useless column adds fog. PCA or selection can help, carefully. Finding a friend in a huge empty stadium versus a small room.\n\nIn the code: a 50-long row of zeros with one 1 at index 3. sum(row) is 1, one tiny signal in 50 numbers.\n\nA common mistake is 200 dummy columns on 30 rows. The model then fits noise in empty space.", code: "# few rows, many zeros (sparse empty space)\nrow = [0] * 50\nrow[3] = 1\nprint(\"one tiny signal in 50 numbers\", sum(row))" },
    { id: 65, level: "advanced", q: "What is a parametric model?", a: "Parametric models have a fixed set of parameters, like linear regression. Non-parametric models can grow with data, like k-NN or a deep tree. Flexibility needs more data.\n\nNeither is always better. Name the trade-off. A mixer with 3 dials versus a mixer that grows extra dials as you cook more.\n\nIn the code: slope 2 and intercept 1. pred is 2*3+1 which is 7. The comment says k-NN would keep all training points instead.\n\nA common mistake is calling neural nets non-parametric because they feel flexible. A fixed-size net still has a fixed number of weights.", code: "# parametric: two numbers only\nslope, intercept = 2.0, 1.0\npred = slope * 3 + intercept\nprint(pred)\n# k-NN would keep all training points instead" },
    { id: 66, level: "intermediate", q: "What is ensemble?", a: "An ensemble mixes models: bagging, boosting, stacking. The group is often better than one. A random forest is bagging of trees.\n\nYou pay with more compute and harder stories. Still start with one simple model. A choir instead of one singer.\n\nIn the code: VotingClassifier wraps LogisticRegression and a depth-2 tree. print shows the ensemble object.\n\nA common mistake is stacking five heavy models before a baseline. You cannot tell if the choir is better than one singer.", code: "from sklearn.ensemble import VotingClassifier\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nens = VotingClassifier([\n    (\"lr\", LogisticRegression()),\n    (\"dt\", DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=2)),\n])\nprint(ens)" },
    { id: 67, level: "beginner", q: "What is bagging?", a: "You train the same kind of model on different random samples. Then you average or vote. That reduces variance, the wiggly part.\n\nRandom forest is bagging plus random features. Asking several groups of students, each who studied a random chapter mix. The average is calmer.\n\nIn the code: votes 0, 0, 1. bagging vote picks 0 because two zeros beat one one.\n\nA common mistake is calling boosting bagging. Boosting is a sequence that fixes leftovers, not a vote of independent copies.", code: "# three models vote\nvotes = [0, 0, 1]\nprint(\"bagging vote\", 1 if votes.count(1) > votes.count(0) else 0)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 68, level: "beginner", q: "What is boosting?", a: "Each new model focuses on the last model's mistakes. It can overfit if you boost too long. Gradient boosting is the famous table-data version.\n\nUse validation to stop. Useful and sharp. A tutor who only drills the questions you got wrong yesterday.\n\nIn the code: true 10, pred 7, residual 3. print leftover for next tree is 3.\n\nA common mistake is boosting until train error is zero. The next trees then chase noise.", code: "# residual picture: next model fits the leftover error\ntrue, pred = 10, 7\nresidual = true - pred\nprint(\"leftover for next tree\", residual)" },
    { id: 69, level: "intermediate", q: "What is early stopping?", a: "You stop when validation loss stops improving. It is common in boosting and neural nets. It reduces overfit from extra rounds.\n\nYou need a validation set for this. Save the best checkpoint. Leaving the party when the fun peaks, not at sunrise.\n\nIn the code: val_loss 0.9, 0.5, 0.4, 0.41, 0.50. The loop tracks best and wait. print shows each step's loss and best so far.\n\nA common mistake is early stopping on train loss. Train can keep falling while valid is already rising.", code: "val_loss = [0.9, 0.5, 0.4, 0.41, 0.50]\nbest, wait = val_loss[0], 0\nfor i, loss in enumerate(val_loss):\n    if loss < best:\n        best, wait = loss, 0\n    else:\n        wait += 1\n    print(i, loss, \"best\", best)" },
    { id: 70, level: "beginner", q: "What is a neural net layer?", a: "A layer is a set of units. Each unit does a weighted sum plus a non-linear function like ReLU. Stacking layers lets the net learn more complex shapes.\n\nOne layer is a mix; many layers are a deep mix. You still need data. A row of tiny calculators passing notes to the next row.\n\nIn the code: layer sums x * w. With relu True it returns max(0, s). print(layer([1, 2], [0.5, 0.5])) is 1.5.\n\nA common mistake is many linear layers with no ReLU. Then the stack collapses to one big line.", code: "def layer(xs, weights, relu=True):\n    s = sum(x * w for x, w in zip(xs, weights))\n    return max(0, s) if relu else s\nprint(layer([1, 2], [0.5, 0.5]))" },
    { id: 71, level: "intermediate", q: "What is ReLU?", a: "ReLU is max(0, x): negatives become 0. It is simple and common. It lets deep nets learn non-straight patterns.\n\nWithout some non-linear door, many layers collapse to one line. There are other activations too. A one-way door: positive signals pass, negative ones stop.\n\nIn the code: relu(3) is 3. relu(-2) is 0.\n\nA common mistake is using ReLU on the last layer of a binary probability. You usually want a sigmoid or softmax there.", code: "def relu(x):\n    return max(0, x)\nprint(relu(3), relu(-2))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 72, level: "intermediate", q: "What is softmax?", a: "Softmax turns a list of numbers into probabilities that add to 1. It is often the last layer for multi-class classification. The biggest number becomes the biggest probability.\n\nYou then pick the largest slice. It is not used for a single yes/no the same way as sigmoid. Slicing a pizza so slices add to a whole pizza.\n\nIn the code: logits 1.0, 2.0, 0.0. exp then divide by the sum. print shows three probabilities that add to 1.\n\nA common mistake is softmax on one number. You need a list of class scores.", code: "import math\nlogits = [1.0, 2.0, 0.0]\nexps = [math.exp(z) for z in logits]\ns = sum(exps)\nprint([e / s for e in exps])" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "What is overfitting in a neural net?", a: "The net memorizes train examples. Fight with more data, dropout, weight decay, augmentation, early stopping. Watch train vs validation loss.\n\nIf train drops and valid rises, stop. A student who memorized every pixel of the homework photo. A new photo of the same cat fails.\n\nIn the code: train_loss falls 0.8, 0.3, 0.05. val_loss goes 0.8, 0.4, 0.9. print says train down, valid up -> overfit.\n\nA common mistake is more epochs because train loss is still falling. Valid already told you to stop.", code: "train_loss = [0.8, 0.3, 0.05]\nval_loss = [0.8, 0.4, 0.9]\nprint(\"train down, valid up -> overfit\")\nprint(list(zip(train_loss, val_loss)))" },
    { id: 74, level: "beginner", q: "What is dropout?", a: "During training, dropout randomly turns off some units. The net cannot rely on one path. It is a regularizer for nets.\n\nAt predict time, dropout is usually off. Practicing a play with a random actor missing each rehearsal. The team learns to share the work.\n\nIn the code: units 0.5, 0.2, 0.9. seed 0. each unit becomes 0 if a random draw is < 0.5. print shows the dropped vector.\n\nA common mistake is leaving dropout on at predict time without the right scaling. Scores then jump run to run.", code: "import random\nunits = [0.5, 0.2, 0.9]\nrandom.seed(0)\ndropped = [0.0 if random.random() < 0.5 else u for u in units]\nprint(\"train with dropout\", dropped)" },
    { id: 75, level: "intermediate", q: "CPU vs GPU for ML?", a: "Classic sklearn on a table is fine on CPU. Big neural nets do lots of matrix math; GPU is much faster. You can learn ML without a GPU.\n\nBuy or rent a GPU when the net is slow, not on day one. Data cleaning is still CPU and pandas. A bicycle for a village and a truck for a mountain of boxes.\n\nIn the code: small_table is 2x2. print sklearn-size. The next print says a big net would multiply huge matrices on GPU.\n\nA common mistake is waiting to start until you own a GPU. pandas and sklearn do not need one.", code: "# picture: small table vs big matrix\nsmall_table = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nprint(\"sklearn-size\", small_table)\nprint(\"big net would multiply huge matrices on GPU\")" },
    { id: 76, level: "beginner", q: "What is a model file?", a: "After fit, you save the model object. Another program loads it and calls predict. Version the model with the code and the data date.\n\njoblib and pickle are common for sklearn. Do not load files you do not trust. Saving a recipe card after you tasted it.\n\nIn the code: LinearRegression fits [[1],[2]] to [1,2]. saved stores coef and intercept as plain numbers. print toy model file.\n\nA common mistake is pickling a model and forgetting the scaler. Inference then uses raw numbers the model never saw.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit([[1], [2]], [1, 2])\nsaved = {\"coef\": m.coef_.tolist(), \"intercept\": float(m.intercept_)}\nprint(\"toy model file\", saved)" },
    { id: 77, level: "intermediate", q: "Training vs inference?", a: "Training learns weights; it is slow and needs labels. Inference is predict on new rows; it should be fast. Inference must use the same preprocessing.\n\nA mismatch in scaling at inference quietly ruins scores. Pipelines help. School versus doing the job.\n\nIn the code: Pipeline StandardScaler then LinearRegression. fit on 1 and 10. inference predict([[4.0]]) applies the same scaler then the line.\n\nA common mistake is scaling train in the notebook and forgetting to scale live requests. Production scores then collapse.", code: "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\npipe = Pipeline([(\"sc\", StandardScaler()), (\"m\", LinearRegression())])\npipe.fit([[1.0], [10.0]], [1.0, 10.0])\nprint(\"inference\", pipe.predict([[4.0]]))" },
    { id: 78, level: "advanced", q: "What is MLOps in one line?", a: "MLOps means version data, retrain, watch live scores, and roll back a bad model. It is DevOps ideas for ML. A notebook is not a production system.\n\nMonitoring matters because the world changes. Say data + model + code versions together. A bakery that checks bread every hour, not one lucky loaf.\n\nIn the code: registry model price_v3, data_date 2026-09-01, metric_live 0.12. print shows that record.\n\nA common mistake is deploying a notebook cell and never watching the live metric. Drift then happens in silence.", code: "registry = {\n    \"model\": \"price_v3\",\n    \"data_date\": \"2026-09-01\",\n    \"metric_live\": 0.12,\n}\nprint(registry)" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "Why can production scores drop?", a: "The world changed, data drift, the pipeline broke, or the notebook leaked and never had real skill. Monitor inputs and metrics. Alerts on input ranges catch many breaks.\n\nRetrain with recent honest data. Yesterday's model drives into a wall after new roads. A map of a city after new roads are built.\n\nIn the code: train_mean 50, live_mean 80. print input shift 30. If abs shift > 10, print check data drift.\n\nA common mistake is blaming the algorithm first. Often a column type changed or a join broke.", code: "train_mean = 50\nlive_mean = 80\nprint(\"input shift\", live_mean - train_mean)\nif abs(live_mean - train_mean) > 10:\n    print(\"check data drift\")" },
    { id: 80, level: "beginner", q: "What is data drift?", a: "New users, new prices, new words. The old model quietly gets worse. You watch feature stats and live metrics.\n\nDrift is normal; ignoring it is the bug. Plan a retrain. A clothing shop using last winter's sizes in a heat wave.\n\nIn the code: old_words sale, offer. new_words sale, crypto, airdrop. print new tokens is crypto and airdrop.\n\nA common mistake is retraining on the same old snapshot. The model never sees the new words.", code: "old_words = {\"sale\", \"offer\"}\nnew_words = {\"sale\", \"crypto\", \"airdrop\"}\nprint(\"new tokens\", new_words - old_words)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 81, level: "beginner", q: "Can ML work with little data?", a: "A simple model or a pre-trained net, transfer learning, can help. If you have 30 rows and 50 features, be humble and use a baseline. More features than rows is a red flag.\n\nRegularize, simplify, or get more data. Do not claim magic. Guessing a whole city from three postcards.\n\nIn the code: n_rows 30, n_features 50. print those counts. If features > rows, print keep a tiny model or get more rows.\n\nA common mistake is a deep net on 30 rows. You will overfit the postcards.", code: "n_rows, n_features = 30, 50\nprint(\"rows\", n_rows, \"features\", n_features)\nif n_features > n_rows:\n    print(\"keep a tiny model or get more rows\")" },
    { id: 82, level: "intermediate", q: "What is transfer learning?", a: "You start from a model trained on a huge dataset, like many images. Then you adapt it to your smaller task. This is common in deep learning.\n\nYou still need some labeled examples. Classic sklearn tables usually do not use this word. A chef who already knows sauces, then learns your one new dish.\n\nIn the code: base_features from a big net. weight 0.5 on a small last layer. print adapt of the sum times weight.\n\nA common mistake is transfer learning talk on a 10-column spreadsheet. There is no huge pretrained net for that table.", code: "# picture: reuse a trained feature block\nbase_features = [0.1, 0.8, 0.0]   # from a big net\n# your small last layer\nweight = 0.5\nprint(\"adapt\", sum(base_features) * weight)" },
    { id: 83, level: "beginner", q: "What is NLP?", a: "NLP is machine learning on language: spam, sentiment, search, chatbots. Beginners start with bag-of-words counts. Later come transformers.\n\nText must become numbers first. That step is the whole game. Teaching a computer to sort mail by the words inside.\n\nIn the code: text buy now cheap cheap. Counter(text.split()) counts each word. cheap appears twice.\n\nA common mistake is feeding raw strings into a linear model. The model needs numbers, not English letters.", code: "text = \"buy now cheap cheap\"\n# bag of words\nfrom collections import Counter\nprint(Counter(text.split()))" },
    { id: 84, level: "beginner", q: "What is computer vision?", a: "Computer vision is ML on images and video. Classify a photo, find a face, read a digit. Today this is mostly deep learning.\n\nPixels are numbers in a grid. Start with a tiny grayscale example in your head. Teaching a camera to name what it sees.\n\nIn the code: image is 2x2 grayscale [[0, 255], [255, 0]]. print shows those pixel numbers.\n\nA common mistake is flattening a photo into 3 million features on 40 rows with no pretrained net. You will overfit pixels.", code: "# 2x2 grayscale image as numbers\nimage = [[0, 255], [255, 0]]\nprint(image)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 85, level: "beginner", q: "What is a recommendation system (simple)?", a: "A simple start is popular items, or users who liked X also liked Y. Harder methods use matrix math or two-tower nets. Cold start is hard: new users have no history.\n\nBegin with a popular list plus one similarity rule. Metrics and fairness still matter. A shopkeeper who remembers what pairs sold together.\n\nIn the code: Ada liked tea and biscuits. Lin liked tea and cake. union prints tea, biscuits, cake as people who liked tea also liked.\n\nA common mistake is only popular items. Then new niche users always see the same five hits.", code: "liked = {\"Ada\": {\"tea\", \"biscuits\"}, \"Lin\": {\"tea\", \"cake\"}}\n# people who liked tea also liked...\nprint(liked[\"Ada\"] | liked[\"Lin\"])\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 86, level: "intermediate", q: "What is an embedding?", a: "An embedding is a list of numbers for a thing. Similar things sit close in that space. Used in NLP and recommenders.\n\nYou do not read the numbers as English. Distance has meaning. A map pin for each word; synonyms are nearby towns.\n\nIn the code: cat [0.9, 0.1], dog [0.8, 0.2], car [0.1, 0.9]. dist is sum of squared differences. cat-dog is smaller than cat-car.\n\nA common mistake is averaging embedding numbers as if they were scores. The meaning is position, not the raw average.", code: "cat = [0.9, 0.1]\ndog = [0.8, 0.2]\ncar = [0.1, 0.9]\ndef dist(a, b):\n    return sum((x - y) ** 2 for x, y in zip(a, b))\nprint(\"cat-dog\", dist(cat, dog), \"cat-car\", dist(cat, car))" },
    { id: 87, level: "beginner", q: "What should you do before any model?", a: "Define the question. Look at the data. Clean it. Pick a metric that matches the business. Set a baseline. Then model.\n\nSkipping this is how people fit noise. Write the decision you will make with the score. Then code. Measuring the room before buying a carpet.\n\nIn the code: steps question, look, clean, metric, baseline, model. join prints that order with arrows.\n\nA common mistake is picking XGBoost in sentence one. Then you never defined success.", code: "steps = [\n    \"question\",\n    \"look\",\n    \"clean\",\n    \"metric\",\n    \"baseline\",\n    \"model\",\n]\nprint(\" -> \".join(steps))" },
    { id: 88, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you explain a linear model to a manager?", a: "Each feature has a weight. If size goes up by 1, predicted price changes by that weight, holding others fixed. Show MAE in money, not only R².\n\nSay the holding others fixed line; it is often false in real life but is the model story. Do not claim cause unless you designed for cause. A simple recipe card: plus this, minus that.\n\nIn the code: size_w 0.4, rooms_w 2.0, b 5.0. size 80 rooms 2. pred is 0.4*80 + 2*2 + 5. print predicted price and a reminder about MAE in rupees.\n\nA common mistake is reading a weight as this feature causes the outcome. The model only correlates in the table.", code: "size_w, rooms_w, b = 0.4, 2.0, 5.0\nsize, rooms = 80, 2\npred = size_w * size + rooms_w * rooms + b\nprint(\"predicted price\", pred)\nprint(\"MAE story in rupees, not only R2\")" },
    { id: 89, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you explain a tree?", a: "You follow one path: if size > 1000 and city is Pune then. Feature importance is a hint, not proof of cause. Show one path with real numbers.\n\nKeep depth small if a person must trust it. Forests are harder; explain one tree first. A clinic triage form.\n\nIn the code: tree(size) returns cheap if size < 50 else pricey. print tree(30) cheap and tree(80) pricey.\n\nA common mistake is dumping 200 trees on a manager. Walk one path instead.", code: "def tree(size):\n    if size < 50:\n        return \"cheap\"\n    return \"pricey\"\nprint(tree(30), tree(80))" },
    { id: 90, level: "advanced", q: "What is SHAP (high level)?", a: "SHAP estimates how much each feature pushed this one guess up or down. It is nicer than raw importance for a story. It is still not magic causation.\n\nUse it to debug, not to prove fairness by itself. Splitting a restaurant bill by who ordered what. Mention it if asked about explainability.\n\nIn the code: base 20. pushes size +8, age -2. prediction is 26. print the pushes dict.\n\nA common mistake is treating SHAP as a court-ready cause. It explains the model's arithmetic, not the world.", code: "# toy \"push\" picture for one prediction\nbase = 20\npushes = {\"size\": +8, \"age\": -2}\nprint(\"prediction\", base + sum(pushes.values()))\nprint(pushes)" },
    { id: 91, level: "beginner", q: "Is more data always better?", a: "More good, relevant data usually helps. More of the same bias, or leaked columns, does not. Quality and the right split matter.\n\nYou still need labels you trust. A small clean set can beat a huge dirty set. More copies of a wrong map.\n\nIn the code: good 100, leaky 10000. print says 100 honest rows beat 10000 leaked rows.\n\nA common mistake is scraping a huge table with a leaked target column. Size hid the cheat.", code: "good = 100\nleaky = 10000\nprint(\"100 honest rows beat 10000 leaked rows\")\nprint(\"good\", good, \"leaky\", leaky)" },
    { id: 92, level: "beginner", q: "Do you need calculus to start ML?", a: "To use sklearn on a table, no. To feel gradient descent, a tiny slope idea helps. Learn by doing first, then the math.\n\nYou can say we step downhill on the loss without writing integrals. Grow math as questions get deeper. Driving before you build an engine.\n\nIn the code: if loss rises when w rises, decrease w. The comment is the slope idea.\n\nA common mistake is waiting months to start until calculus feels done. You can fit LinearRegression today.", code: "# slope idea: if w goes up, loss goes up, so walk w down\nw, loss_if_w_up = 1.0, \"higher\"\nprint(\"if loss rises when w rises, decrease w\")\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 93, level: "beginner", q: "Python libraries a beginner should name?", a: "NumPy, pandas, matplotlib or seaborn, scikit-learn. Later: PyTorch or TensorFlow. Jupyter is for exploration.\n\nYou do not need all of them on day one. sklearn + pandas is a strong pair. A starter kit, not the whole internet.\n\nIn the code: names numpy, pandas, matplotlib, sklearn. join prints them with commas.\n\nA common mistake is listing twenty libraries you have not used. Name the ones you can walk through.", code: "names = [\"numpy\", \"pandas\", \"matplotlib\", \"sklearn\"]\nprint(\", \".join(names))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "Notebook vs script?", a: "Notebooks are for plots and poking. A .py script or pipeline is for a repeatable train/predict job. Both are used.\n\nCopy the winning cells into a script when it matters. Do not ship a 200-cell notebook as production. A sketchbook versus a printed SOP.\n\nIn the code: train(X, y) fits LinearRegression and returns the model. print predict([[3]]) after fitting [[1],[2]] to [1,2].\n\nA common mistake is production that is a notebook you must Run All in order. One missed cell then trains the wrong data.", code: "# script-shaped training\ndef train(X, y):\n    from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n    return LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint(train([[1], [2]], [1, 2]).predict([[3]]))" },
    { id: 95, level: "intermediate", q: "Why is random_state on the split important in a tutorial?", a: "So you and the teacher get the same rows and can compare numbers. In real life you still need an honest future or extra test. It is a teaching tool.\n\nDo not worship 42. Say both things in an interview. Using the same practice paper so answers match the key.\n\nIn the code: train_test_split with random_state 0 prints the first train chunk. That chunk repeats every run.\n\nA common mistake is thinking a tutorial score is your production skill. The seed only matched the lesson.", code: "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nX, y = [[1], [2], [3], [4]], [0, 1, 0, 1]\nprint(train_test_split(X, y, random_state=0)[0])\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 96, level: "advanced", q: "What is a leakage example with dates?", a: "Using days until event computed with an event date that happens after you must predict. Or shuffling a time series so the future mixes into train. If a field needs the future, it is not a legal feature.\n\nTime split first. Then build features only from the past. Predicting a cricket score using the final scoreboard.\n\nIn the code: today 1, 2 and event_day 10, 10. days_until is event_day minus today. print says this feature peeks at the future.\n\nA common mistake is a feature named days_to_churn that used the churn timestamp. The model never had that at score time.", code: "import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"today\": [1, 2],\n    \"event_day\": [10, 10],\n})\n# leak: event_day is in the future\ndf[\"days_until\"] = df[\"event_day\"] - df[\"today\"]\nprint(\"this feature peeks at the future\")\nprint(df)" },
    { id: 97, level: "intermediate", q: "Classification on 3+ classes?", a: "That is multi-class. Models can use one-vs-rest or a native multi-class loss. Metric: accuracy, or macro-F1 if classes are uneven.\n\nConfusion matrix becomes bigger. Still start with a baseline of the most common class. Sorting mail into bills, ads, and personal.\n\nIn the code: y is 0, 1, 2 repeating. LogisticRegression fits X 0, 1, 2. predict([[1]]) is class 1.\n\nA common mistake is binary precision/recall without saying which class, then applying it to three classes with no macro or per-class table.", code: "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nX = [[0], [1], [2], [0], [1], [2]]\ny = [0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2]\nm = LogisticRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint(m.predict([[1]]))" },
    { id: 98, level: "beginner", q: "What is unsupervised clustering used for?", a: "You find groups you did not label: customer segments, topics in text. You still have to interpret the groups. k-means is the first tool people name.\n\nThere is no accuracy against y, because there is no y. Use plots and stories to check if groups make sense. Sorting a mixed box of Lego by color without a chart.\n\nIn the code: X is 1, 1.2, 8, 8.1. KMeans n_clusters 2 fit_predict prints group ids for those four points.\n\nA common mistake is reporting clustering accuracy against labels you were not supposed to use. Then it was supervised in disguise.", code: "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nX = [[1], [1.2], [8], [8.1]]\nprint(KMeans(n_clusters=2, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit_predict(X))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 99, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you pick k in k-means?", a: "You try several k. People look at an elbow of inertia or a silhouette score. There is no perfect k from a formula.\n\nPick one you can explain to a human. Too many boxes is as useless as one box. Choosing how many boxes to sort toys into.\n\nIn the code: six points in three pairs. k=2 and k=3 each print inertia. You compare those numbers by eye.\n\nA common mistake is k equal to the number of rows. Then every point is its own group and clustering did nothing.", code: "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nX = [[1], [1.1], [5], [5.2], [9], [9.1]]\nfor k in [2, 3]:\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(X)\n    print(k, \"inertia\", km.inertia_)" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you answer an ML case study?", a: "Clarify the decision and the metric. Discuss data, leakage, and a simple baseline. Then a model, then how you would validate and deploy.\n\nSay what you would not claim. Structure beats buzzwords. A doctor: symptoms, tests, treatment, follow-up, and honesty about limits.\n\nIn the code: talk is decision + metric, data + leakage, baseline, model, validate, deploy + monitor, limits. join prints that path.\n\nA common mistake is jumping to a neural net in sentence two. Interviewers want the decision and the leak checks first.", code: "talk = [\n    \"decision + metric\",\n    \"data + leakage\",\n    \"baseline\",\n    \"model\",\n    \"validate\",\n    \"deploy + monitor\",\n    \"limits\",\n]\nprint(\" -> \".join(talk))" }
  ]
};
